---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj26.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Including Japan's Distinctive Lump-Sum Retirement Allowances" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Retirement Benefit Obligation" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Plan Assets and the Net Recognised Liability/Asset" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Components of Retirement Benefit Cost" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Discount Rate Determination" },
        { id: "s7", label: "The Consolidated/Individual Divergence — Recap From Statement No. 19" },
        { id: "s8", label: "The Simplified Method for Smaller Enterprises" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Calculating the Net Recognised Liability" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example — the Simplified Method in Practice" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="hub">
      <div class="hub-box">
        <div class="hub-title">ASBJ Statement No. 26 — Accounting Standard for Retirement Benefits</div>
        <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan GAAP (J-GAAP) · 退職給付に関する会計基準 · The comprehensive standard culminating Statements No. 3, 14, and 19 · JPY examples</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The comprehensive destination of a decades-long staged reform</div>
        <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 26 is the CURRENT, COMPREHENSIVE J-GAAP standard for retirement benefit accounting — the CULMINATION of the staged reform process traced through Statements No. 3, No. 14 (both now ABOLISHED), and No. 19 (still integrated, see companion posters). It governs the RECOGNITION and MEASUREMENT of an entity's retirement benefit OBLIGATION, PLAN ASSETS, and the resulting NET liability (or asset) recognised on the Balance Sheet.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Including Japan's Distinctive Lump-Sum Retirement Allowances</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Both pension arrangements and Japan's traditional lump-sum system</div>
        <div class="principle">This Statement applies to companies sponsoring RETIREMENT BENEFIT plans — INCLUDING both Western-style DEFINED BENEFIT PENSION arrangements AND Japan's DISTINCTIVELY COMMON "LUMP-SUM RETIREMENT ALLOWANCE" (退職一時金) system — a SINGLE lump-sum payment at retirement, typically based on FINAL salary and YEARS of service — which is accounted for under the SAME defined benefit framework as a formal pension plan, requiring FULL actuarial valuation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Retirement Benefit Obligation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The actuarial present value of benefits attributable to service to date</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The RETIREMENT BENEFIT OBLIGATION (退職給付債務) is the ACTUARIAL PRESENT VALUE of retirement benefits ATTRIBUTABLE to employee SERVICE rendered UP TO the reporting date — reflecting assumptions about FUTURE salary increases, employee TURNOVER, mortality, and the DISCOUNT rate (Section 6) — a forward-looking, ACTUARIALLY determined figure, NOT simply the amount that would be payable if all employees resigned TODAY.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Plan Assets and the Net Recognised Liability/Asset</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Netting the obligation against any funded plan assets</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where an entity FUNDS its retirement benefit obligation through a SEPARATE pension trust or fund, PLAN ASSETS are measured at FAIR VALUE. The Balance Sheet recognises a SINGLE NET figure: the retirement benefit OBLIGATION LESS plan assets — a NET LIABILITY where the obligation EXCEEDS plan assets (the typical case), or a NET ASSET in the LESS common case where plan assets EXCEED the obligation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Components of Retirement Benefit Cost</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Service cost, interest cost, and expected return on plan assets</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-2">
            <span>Component</span>
            <span>Description</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Service cost</span><span>The increase in the obligation attributable to employee service rendered DURING the current period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>Interest cost</span><span>The increase in the obligation reflecting the passage of time (unwinding the discount)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Expected return on plan assets</span><span>The anticipated investment return on funded plan assets, offsetting the above costs</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Discount Rate Determination</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">High-quality corporate bond yields matching the obligation's duration</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The DISCOUNT RATE used to determine the present value of the retirement benefit obligation is based on the YIELD of HIGH-QUALITY corporate bonds (or, in some cases, government bonds), with a DURATION broadly MATCHING the estimated TIMING of future benefit payments — reflecting the TIME VALUE of money appropriate to the obligation's OWN expected payment profile.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Consolidated/Individual Divergence — Recap From Statement No. 19</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A live, current feature of today's standard</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">As established through Statement No. 19 (see the companion poster), ACTUARIAL gains/losses and PRIOR SERVICE COST are recognised DIFFERENTLY depending on WHICH set of financial statements is being prepared: IMMEDIATE recognition within Net Assets (later recycled) for CONSOLIDATED purposes, but GRADUAL amortisation through profit or loss for INDIVIDUAL (non-consolidated) purposes — a distinctive, STILL-CURRENT feature of this comprehensive standard.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>The Simplified Method for Smaller Enterprises</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">A formula-based alternative to full actuarial valuation</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">For SMALLER enterprises (generally those below a specified EMPLOYEE count threshold), this Statement permits a "SIMPLIFIED METHOD" — estimating the retirement benefit obligation using a FORMULA-BASED approach (e.g. the amount that would be PAYABLE if all eligible employees VOLUNTARILY resigned at the reporting date, adjusted by a standard FACTOR) RATHER than requiring a FULL, individualised actuarial valuation — a genuine, SIZE-BASED practical expedient recognising that full actuarial valuations can be DISPROPORTIONATELY costly for smaller employers.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Calculating the Net Recognised Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Netting the obligation against plan assets</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A funded pension arrangement</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company's retirement benefit obligation is ¥3,200,000,000. Plan assets held in a separate pension trust have a fair value of ¥2,500,000,000.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The company recognises a NET RETIREMENT BENEFIT LIABILITY of ¥3,200,000,000 − ¥2,500,000,000 = <strong>¥700,000,000</strong> on the Balance Sheet — a SINGLE net figure, rather than presenting the obligation and plan assets as separate gross line items.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — the Simplified Method in Practice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A formula-based estimate for a small employer</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A small company's lump-sum retirement allowance liability</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A small company with 15 employees estimates that, if all eligible employees voluntarily resigned today, it would owe ¥120,000,000 in lump-sum retirement allowances. It applies a standard adjustment factor of 0.9 under the simplified method.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Estimated retirement benefit obligation = ¥120,000,000 × 0.9 = <strong>¥108,000,000</strong> — a REASONABLE APPROXIMATION obtained WITHOUT the cost of a FULL, individualised actuarial valuation, appropriate given the company's SMALL size.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 26 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Movement in the retirement benefit obligation and plan assets during the period</span>
          <span>Components of retirement benefit cost recognised</span>
          <span>Key actuarial assumptions, including the discount rate</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether an enterprise qualifies for, and should elect, the simplified method</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Discount rate, expected future salary increases, employee turnover, and mortality assumptions</span>
              <span>Expected return on plan assets</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP (consolidated) vs IAS 19</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-12-12">
            <span>Feature</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 26, consolidated)</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 19)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Net liability recognition</span><span>Obligation less plan assets, on the Balance Sheet</span><span>Same net approach — converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Actuarial gain/loss recognition (consolidated)</span><span>Immediate, within Net Assets</span><span>Immediate, within OCI — converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Individual (separate) statement treatment</span><span>Gradual, deferred amortisation — a distinctive divergence</span><span>No equivalent separate-statement distinction</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">At the CONSOLIDATED level, this Statement is genuinely CONVERGED with modern IAS 19 — the CONTINUING individual-statement divergence (Section 7) is the standard's most distinctive remaining feature.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every retirement benefit event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14">
            <span>Event</span>
            <span>Statement(s) affected</span>
            <span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Net retirement benefit liability/asset</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Obligation less plan assets</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Retirement benefit cost</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Service cost, interest cost, expected return on plan assets</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2">
            <span>Standard</span>
            <span>Connection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 19</span><span>Supplies the consolidated/individual dual-track treatment of actuarial gains/losses this Statement incorporates</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 25</span><span>Presentation of Comprehensive Income — supplies the consolidated Net Assets presentation for retirement benefit remeasurements</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Lump-sum retirement allowance (退職一時金)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A single lump-sum payment made at employee retirement, typically based on final salary and years of service — a distinctively common Japanese retirement benefit arrangement.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
          <div class="glossary-term">Simplified method</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A formula-based estimation approach for the retirement benefit obligation, available to smaller enterprises in place of a full actuarial valuation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)
        <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)
        <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部
        <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)
        <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="footer-note">ASBJ Statement No. 26 — Accounting Standard for Retirement Benefits · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>

</StandardPageLayout>
